# The Next.js storefront lives at the repository root (src/). Several unrelated
# sibling projects share this repo — the platform monorepo, the React Native
# app, and the device firmware — and none of them are imported by the site.
#
# Vercel only skips node_modules at the top level, so a CLI deploy was uploading
# roughly 6 GB of their locally-installed dependencies. That pushed a single
# serverless function to 932 MB against a 250 MB limit and failed the deploy.
# Git-triggered deploys never hit this because those directories' dependencies
# are not committed.
#
# THE LEADING SLASH IS LOAD-BEARING.
#
# These patterns are gitignore syntax, so an unanchored `platform/` matches a
# directory of that name at ANY depth — including `src/app/.../admin/platform`.
# Three real routes were silently missing from production because of it:
#
#   src/app/smarthome/admin/platform   the platform admin page  (404)
#   src/app/smarthome/firmware         the firmware console     (404)
#   src/app/api/devices/firmware       the OTA manifest         (404)
#
# The last one is the one that hurt. It is the endpoint `checkOTA()` calls, so
# every device asking "is there a newer build for me?" got a 404 and concluded
# there was not. OTA appeared to work only because we had been pushing updates
# by hand over MQTT, which takes a different path entirely.
#
# Nothing reports this. The build succeeds, the deploy succeeds, and the routes
# simply are not there — so anchor every one of these to the repository root.

**/node_modules

/*
 * A failed install can leave a renamed copy behind — `node_modules_stale_…`
 * does not match `**​/node_modules`, so it was uploaded, and one file inside it
 * was locked hard enough to fail the deploy with EPERM. Nothing here is ever
 * wanted in a deployment whatever it is called.
 */
**/node_modules_*
**/.protobufjs-*

/circuvent-platform/
/mobile/
/firmware/
/hardware/
/platform/

# Local build output of the sibling projects.
**/dist
**/.turbo
**/.expo

# Secrets.
#
# This file *replaces* .gitignore for uploads -- Vercel does not consult both --
# so the `.env*` rule in .gitignore does not apply here. Without these lines
# `vercel deploy` uploads real env files into the deployment source.
#
# Two separate problems, and the second is easy to miss: the credentials end up
# in the deployment, *and* Next loads an uploaded .env during the build, so
# local values silently win over the ones configured on the project -- which is
# how a production build ends up pointing at a dev database or localhost URL.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example